% ============================================================================
% other-vowel-team-sound — a second, distinct family of vowel-team spellings
% (a repeated short /oo/, a long /ū/, a glided-toward-w /aw/, and two
% short-vowel exceptions) and the single sound each one represents
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE). A K-2 phonics library, grounding
% ADJ-STDLIB-COVERAGE.md §5.1's "phonics, decoding" Major Gap with the FIFTH
% UFLI phonics unit shipped in this stdlib, and the unit `long-vowel-team-
% sound.adj`'s own header already named and flagged as a future extension.
% ============================================================================
% UFLI's own scope and sequence names this unit, verbatim, "Other Vowel
% Teams" — the direct sibling to `long-vowel-team-sound.adj`'s "Long Vowel
% Teams" unit, immediately following it in lesson order (84-88, then 89-94),
% on the SAME toolbox page family. Where `long-vowel-team-sound.adj` tables
% vowel teams that spell the LONG sound of the FIRST vowel in the pair (ai,
% ee, oa, ie...), this unit's five lessons cover vowel teams that do NOT fit
% that pattern: a repeated short /oo/ (as in "book"), a genuinely different
% "long" vowel sound /ū/ spelled four different ways, a glided-toward-w /aw/
% sound, and — the unit's own final lesson — two spellings that keep their
% familiar letters but take on a SHORT-vowel reading instead of a long one.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? other_vowel_team_sound(au, $Sound)
%       % aw_sound
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `other_vowel_team_sound(spelling, sound)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the sound WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any spelling the cited page's lesson table does
% not name. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a sound and recall EVERY
% spelling that carries it:
%
%     ? other_vowel_team_sound($Sp, long_u_sound)
%       % oo, ew, ui, ue  (all four -- see design note)
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why "oo" carries TWO rows, the SAME one-key/many-values
% shape `digraph-sound.adj`'s own "th" row already established: the cited
% page's lesson table splits "oo" into two separate lessons, one per
% pronunciation —
% lesson 89 gives "oo /oo/" (the short, repeated-vowel sound heard in
% "book") and lesson 90 gives "oo /ū/" (a genuinely different, longer sound
% heard in "moon") — rather than treating "oo" as a single sound the way it
% treats every other spelling here. This table reproduces that split
% faithfully as two rows sharing the same `spelling` key, not collapsed into
% one: a forward query on `oo` therefore yields BOTH rows, an honest
% reflection of the page's lesson table, not an omission.
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why lesson 90's "oo" and lesson 91's "ew"/"ui"/"ue" share
% the SAME sound atom `long_u_sound`: the lesson table's own IPA-ish notation
% writes the identical symbol, "/ū/", for all four spellings across both
% lessons — this table honors that shared notation with one shared sound
% atom, the SAME many-keys-to-one-value shape `long-vowel-team-sound.adj`'s
% own "oa"/"ow_long_o"/"oe" → `long_o_sound` row already established for a
% sound spanning multiple spellings within one lesson; here it spans two
% adjacent lessons instead, which the lesson table's own notation makes
% explicit.
%
% DESIGN NOTE — a genuine "ea" HETERONYM-IN-SPELLING collision with
% `long-vowel-team-sound.adj`, resolved the SAME way that table's own "ow"
% row was, and the exact collision that table's own header already flagged
% by name as a future-round risk: UFLI's cited Other Vowel Teams page tables
% lesson 94 as "ea /ĕ/, a /ŏ/" — the SAME two-letter spelling "ea" that
% `long-vowel-team-sound.adj` already tables (lesson 85 of the neighboring
% "Long Vowel Teams" unit) as the steady LONG-e sound in "team"/"rain". Both
% are real English facts, each tabled by UFLI on its own unit page, about the
% letters — "ea" genuinely IS pronounced two different ways depending on the
% word ("team"/"rain" vs. "bread"/"head") — and UFLI itself teaches them as
% two separate lessons in two separate units. Because these are two
% DIFFERENT predicates (`other_vowel_team_sound/2` here,
% `long_vowel_team_sound/2` there), a bare "ea" key in each would not
% actually conflict at the logic-engine level — SLD resolution scopes ground
% facts by predicate name, not by atom alone — but, per the SAME reasoning
% `long-vowel-team-sound.adj`'s own "ow"/"ow_long_o" design note already
% spelled out, a human or LLM reader scanning row values across the stdlib
% could easily mistake one table's "ea" for the other's without checking
% which predicate governs it. Rather than risk that silent misread, this
% table disambiguates the one genuinely overlapping spelling with an
% explicit `_short_e` suffix on the atom itself, naming which of the two
% tabled senses it stands for right in the key — the identical
% page-driven atom-label convention `long-vowel-team-sound.adj`'s
% `ow_long_o` and `suffix-meaning.adj`'s `_er_agentive` already established.
% A query on the bare atom `ea` against THIS table's predicate honestly
% ABSTAINS — this table asserts nothing about a bare `ea`, so no answer is
% fabricated by accident — while `long_vowel_team_sound(ea, $S)` against the
% OTHER already-shipped table still correctly answers `long_e_sound`,
% exactly as it always has. Empirically verified both tables coexist without
% conflict when imported together (both directions checked against the real
% built CLI before writing this file). The other nine rows in this table
% keep the plain bare-literal-spelling atom convention every sibling UFLI
% table already established, since a full-tree grep (the mandatory
% duplication check before scoping) confirmed none of them — u, oo, ew, ui,
% ue, au, aw, augh, a — collides with any `row` anywhere else in this
% stdlib under a phonics spelling-to-sound predicate; only "ea" needed
% special-casing.
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why the bare atom `a` needs no disambiguation despite being
% reused, as a row value, by several OTHER tables in this stdlib
% (`alphabet.adj`, `vowels.adj`, `morse-code.adj`, `dolch-sight-word-
% level.adj`, `soil-horizons.adj`, `blood-groups.adj`): every one of those
% is a DIFFERENT predicate covering a categorically different fact (a
% letter's alphabet position, an ABO blood-type antigen, a soil horizon
% label...) — none of them is a sibling phonics spelling-to-sound table the
% way `long-vowel-team-sound.adj` is for "ea"/"ow". The disambiguation
% discipline this stdlib has applied so far (`ow_long_o`, `_er_agentive`,
% and this file's own `ea_short_e`) is reserved for a genuine same-KIND
% collision risk — two tables that could plausibly be confused for each
% other by a reader scanning row values in the SAME domain — not for every
% incidental atom reuse across an entire multi-domain stdlib; disambiguating
% every common short atom against the whole stdlib would make every table's
% labels needlessly verbose. `a`'s lesson-94 short-o reading (as in "wasp",
% "wash") carries no such risk here.
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why lesson 92 ("Vowel Teams Review 2") is NOT a row here:
% the page's own lesson table names it a cumulative review lesson, not a new
% spelling-to-sound pairing — the SAME review-lesson exclusion
% `digraph-sound.adj` (lessons 49/53), `diphthong-sound.adj` (its own lesson
% 97), and `long-vowel-team-sound.adj` (its own lesson 88) already
% established for this stdlib.
%
% Provenance — the `source` and the ROWS are not grounded by the same thing,
% and this note says which is which. Both come from the University of
% Florida Literacy Institute (UFLI)'s Foundations Toolbox, "Other Vowel
% Teams Unit Resources (Lessons 89-94)" page — the same source family as
% `digraph-sound.adj`/`diphthong-sound.adj`/`long-vowel-team-sound.adj`/`silent-letter-sound.adj`,
% the direct sequel to `long-vowel-team-sound.adj`'s own cited page —
% curl-fetched directly rather than through an AI-summarized WebFetch.
%
% The `source` is that page's opening definition paragraph, quoted WHOLE and
% byte for byte: "A vowel team is a combination of letters that represents a
% vowel sound. These lessons focus on vowel teams that represent a new sound
% (e.g., draw, book) and vowel teams that include letters that aren’t vowels
% (e.g., dew, high). These lessons are designed to build students’ accuracy
% and automaticity in connecting these vowel teams with the sounds
% associated with them. The lessons also build students’ proficiency in
% reading and spelling words that contain these vowel teams."
%
% It says what a vowel team IS and which vowel teams the unit covers. It
% names no spelling and no sound, so it grounds none of the eleven rows.
% Those come from the unit's lesson table, whose "Concept" column names each
% lesson's spellings with their sound, in macron/breve-marked notation — the
% page's own, not IPA. Those cells extract as SEPARATE BLOCKS, one per
% cell, which is exactly the cross-block/table-row class held open on #14111
% ("is a table row a verbatim span?"), so they are deliberately NOT
% presented here as a quoted span, and each row below names the cell it
% reads instead.
%
% Until installment 4g the `source` field carried the definition sentences
% with a hand-assembled rendering of that table STITCHED ONTO THEM, a string
% that appears nowhere on the page: it invented a colon after the unit
% heading, invented a separator between each lesson number and the previous
% cell, dropped the paragraph's two closing sentences, and flattened the
% page's U+2019 apostrophe to an ASCII one in "aren't" — the one apostrophe
% it did reach. A composition presented as a quotation is a defect in the
% FIELD, not a position on the held question — removing it takes no position
% either way. In this file the stitch also inserted a space the page does
% not have: lesson 91's cell breaks the line after the second comma, so it
% reads "ui /ū/,ue /ū/", and the old value wrote "ui /ū/, ue /ū/".
%
%     spelling (atom label)   sound (atom label)   part of the Concept cell
%     -----------------------  --------------------  --------------------------
%     u                        short_oo_sound         "u /oo/" (lesson 89)
%     oo                       short_oo_sound         "oo /oo/" (lesson 89)
%     oo                       long_u_sound           "oo /ū/" (lesson 90)
%     ew                       long_u_sound           "ew /ū/" (lesson 91)
%     ui                       long_u_sound           "ui /ū/" (lesson 91)
%     ue                       long_u_sound           "ue /ū/" (lesson 91)
%     au                       aw_sound               "au /aw/" (lesson 93)
%     aw                       aw_sound               "aw /aw/" (lesson 93)
%     augh                     aw_sound               "augh /aw/" (lesson 93)
%     ea_short_e               short_e_sound          "ea /ĕ/" (lesson 94 -- disambiguated, see design note)
%     a                        short_o_sound          "a /ŏ/" (lesson 94)
%
% The third column is the part of the Concept cell each row reads, not the
% whole cell. The cells themselves are: lesson 89 is "u /oo/, oo /oo/";
% lesson 90 is "oo /ū/"; lesson 91 is "ew /ū/, ui /ū/,ue /ū/"; lesson 93 is
% "au /aw/, aw /aw/, augh /aw/"; lesson 94 is "ea /ĕ/, a /ŏ/".
%
% `trust authoritative` — UFLI is a university literacy research center
% (University of Florida, .edu), the same tier and same source family
% `digraph-sound.adj`/`diphthong-sound.adj`/`long-vowel-team-sound.adj`/
% `silent-letter-sound.adj` already established for this stdlib. A
% full-tree grep for `row (u,`/`row (oo,`/`row (ew,`/`row (ui,`/`row (ue,`/
% `row (au,`/`row (aw,`/`row (augh,`/`row (ea,`/`row (a,` across the whole
% shipped stdlib (the mandatory duplication check before scoping) found zero
% collisions under any phonics spelling-to-sound predicate except the one
% already-documented, already-flagged "ea" case against `long-vowel-team-
% sound.adj`, handled above. This also resolves, in the SAME round, the
% converse of two abstentions `long-vowel-team-sound.adj`'s own header
% already documented: "au"/"aw" were deliberately NOT rows there because
% UFLI tables them under this very unit (lesson 93) — they are genuine rows
% HERE instead, the honest completion of that cross-reference. A spelling
% not one of these eleven — e.g. "ey" (a real vowel-team spelling, but
% WebFetch-confirmed tabled by UFLI under the separate Long Vowel Teams
% lesson 85, not this cited Other Vowel Teams page) — is deliberately NOT a
% row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a sound. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table other_vowel_team_sound {
    columns spelling, sound

    row (u, short_oo_sound)       % lesson 89 cell: "u /oo/, oo /oo/"
    row (oo, short_oo_sound)      % lesson 89 cell: "u /oo/, oo /oo/"
    row (oo, long_u_sound)        % lesson 90 cell: "oo /ū/"
    row (ew, long_u_sound)        % lesson 91 cell: "ew /ū/, ui /ū/,ue /ū/"
    row (ui, long_u_sound)        % lesson 91 cell: "ew /ū/, ui /ū/,ue /ū/"
    row (ue, long_u_sound)        % lesson 91 cell: "ew /ū/, ui /ū/,ue /ū/"
    row (au, aw_sound)            % lesson 93 cell: "au /aw/, aw /aw/, augh /aw/"
    row (aw, aw_sound)            % lesson 93 cell: "au /aw/, aw /aw/, augh /aw/"
    row (augh, aw_sound)          % lesson 93 cell: "au /aw/, aw /aw/, augh /aw/"
    row (ea_short_e, short_e_sound) % lesson 94 cell: "ea /ĕ/, a /ŏ/"
    row (a, short_o_sound)        % lesson 94 cell: "ea /ĕ/, a /ŏ/"

    source "A vowel team is a combination of letters that represents a vowel sound. These lessons focus on vowel teams that represent a new sound (e.g., draw, book) and vowel teams that include letters that aren’t vowels (e.g., dew, high). These lessons are designed to build students’ accuracy and automaticity in connecting these vowel teams with the sounds associated with them. The lessons also build students’ proficiency in reading and spelling words that contain these vowel teams."
    locator "https://ufli.education.ufl.edu/foundations/toolbox/89-94/"
    trust authoritative
}
